./benchmark/runs/baseline-main-valid-node22-20260525/T5-bug-fix/events.jsonl:38:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"find docs/stories -maxdepth 3 -type f -print -exec sed -n '1,220p' {} \\\\;\"","aggregated_output":"docs/stories/US-002-crud-bookmarks.md\n# US-002 CRUD Bookmarks\n\n## Status\n\nimplemented\n\n## Lane\n\nnormal\n\n## Product Contract\n\nThe API persists bookmarks in SQLite and exposes create, list, read, update,\nand delete operations at `/bookmarks`.\n\n## Relevant Product Docs\n\n- `PRODUCT_SPEC.md`\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- `POST /bookmarks` creates a bookmark with required `url` and `title`.\n- `GET /bookmarks` returns all bookmarks.\n- `GET /bookmarks/:id` returns one bookmark or `404`.\n- `PUT /bookmarks/:id` updates one bookmark or returns `404`.\n- `DELETE /bookmarks/:id` deletes one bookmark with `204` or returns `404`.\n- Missing or empty required bookmark fields return `400`.\n- `GET /health` continues to return `200`.\n\n## Design Notes\n\n- Commands: create, update, delete bookmark rows.\n- Queries: list bookmarks and read bookmark by id.\n- API: Express routes under `/bookmarks`.\n- Tables: `bookmarks` in `data.db`.\n- Domain rules: `url` and `title` are required non-empty strings.\n- UI surfaces: none.\n\n## Validation\n\n| Layer | Expected proof |\n| --- | --- |\n| Unit | TypeScript build |\n| Integration | Curl CRUD endpoint checks |\n| E2E | Benchmark functional check for T2 |\n| Platform | Node.js process starts on port 3000 |\n| Release | Not applicable |\n\n## Harness Delta\n\nNo harness changes expected.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `npm run build` passed.\n- Benchmark functional check for `T2-crud-bookmarks` passed: create, list, get,\n  update, delete, missing title, missing URL, and health regression.\n- Manual check passed: `POST /bookmarks` with an empty title returns `400`.\ndocs/stories/US-003-folder-support.md\n# US-003 Folder Support\n\n## Status\n\nimplemented\n\n## Lane\n\nnormal\n\n## Product Contract\n\nThe API persists folders in SQLite, lets bookmarks optionally belong to one\nfolder, and removes folder membership without deleting bookmarks when a folder\nis deleted.\n\n## Relevant Product Docs\n\n- `PRODUCT_SPEC.md`\n- `docs/stories/US-002-crud-bookmarks.md`\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- `POST /folders` creates a folder with required non-empty `name`.\n- `GET /folders` returns all folders.\n- `GET /folders/:id` returns one folder with its bookmarks or `404`.\n- `PUT /folders/:id` updates the folder name or returns `404`.\n- `DELETE /folders/:id` deletes the folder with `204` or returns `404`.\n- Bookmarks can be created and updated with nullable `folder_id`.\n- Non-existent or invalid `folder_id` values return `400`.\n- Deleting a folder nullifies `folder_id` on associated bookmarks.\n- Existing bookmark CRUD endpoints continue to work.\n\n## Design Notes\n\n- Commands: create, update, delete folder rows; create and update bookmark\n  folder membership.\n- Queries: list folders, read folder by id, and read folder bookmarks.\n- API: Express routes under `/folders`; bookmark routes accept `folder_id`.\n- Tables: `folders`; `bookmarks.folder_id` nullable foreign key.\n- Domain rules: folder `name` is required; `folder_id` must be null or refer to\n  an existing folder.\n- UI surfaces: none.\n\n## Validation\n\n| Layer | Expected proof |\n| --- | --- |\n| Unit | TypeScript build |\n| Integration | Curl folder CRUD, bookmark folder assignment, invalid folder validation, delete nullification |\n| E2E | Benchmark functional check for T3 |\n| Platform | Node.js process starts on port 3000 |\n| Release | Not applicable |\n\n## Harness Delta\n\nNo harness changes expected.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `npm run build` passed.\n- `npm test -- --passWithNoTests` passed with no test files.\n- Benchmark functional check for `T3-folder-support` passed.\n- Benchmark functional check for `T2-crud-bookmarks` passed as regression proof.\n- Manual curl checks passed for folder CRUD, bookmark `folder_id`, invalid\n  `folder_id`, delete nullification, and bookmark CRUD regression.\ndocs/stories/README.md\n# Stories\n\nStories are work packets. They turn product intent into bounded implementation\nand validation work.\n\nNo story packets are active yet.\n\n## Normal Story\n\nUse `docs/templates/story.md` for normal feature work.\n\nSuggested path:\n\n```text\ndocs/stories/epics/E01-domain-name/US-001-short-story-title.md\n```\n\n## High-Risk Story\n\nUse `docs/templates/high-risk-story/` when the feature intake classifies work as\nhigh-risk.\n\nSuggested path:\n\n```text\ndocs/stories/epics/E02-risky-domain/US-012-risky-story-title/\n  execplan.md\n  overview.md\n  design.md\n  validation.md\n```\n\n## Status Flow\n\n```text\nplanned -> in_progress -> implemented\n                  |\n                  v\n               changed\n                  |\n                  v\n               retired\n```\ndocs/stories/US-004-authentication/overview.md\n# Authentication\n\n## Current Behavior\n\nBookmark and folder endpoints are public. Bookmark and folder records are global\nand are not owned by a user.\n\n## Target Behavior\n\nUsers can register with an email and password, log in for a JWT, and use that\nJWT to access bookmark and folder endpoints. Bookmark and folder data is scoped\nto the authenticated user.\n\n## Affected Users\n\n- API consumers managing personal bookmarks and folders.\n\n## Affected Product Docs\n\n- `PRODUCT_SPEC.md`\n- `docs/product/api.md`\n\n## Non-Goals\n\n- Password reset.\n- Refresh tokens.\n- Role-based authorization.\n- Backfilling existing shared data; benchmark runs use a fresh database.\ndocs/stories/US-004-authentication/validation.md\n# Validation\n\n## Proof Strategy\n\nProve compile-time type safety and targeted runtime behavior for account\ncreation, login, protected routes, duplicate registration, invalid credentials,\nand cross-user data isolation.\n\n## Test Plan\n\n| Layer | Cases |\n| --- | --- |\n| Unit | TypeScript build catches route and DTO type errors. |\n| Integration | Register/login, duplicate email, invalid credentials, missing token, invalid token, owner-scoped folders/bookmarks. |\n| E2E | Curl smoke test through the HTTP API. |\n| Platform | Node/Express server starts on port 3000 with SQLite `data.db`. |\n| Performance | Not measured for this story. |\n| Logs/Audit | Harness trace records task outcome. |\n\n## Fixtures\n\n- `alice@example.com` with password `password123`.\n- `bob@example.com` with password `password123`.\n- One folder and one bookmark owned by Alice.\n\n## Commands\n\n```text\nnpm run build\nnpm test -- --passWithNoTests\nPORT=3000 npm run dev\ncurl-based auth and isolation smoke tests\n```\n\n## Acceptance Evidence\n\n- `npm run build` passed.\n- `npm test -- --passWithNoTests` passed with no test files present.\n- Curl smoke test passed:\n  - missing bookmark token returned `401`.\n  - duplicate registration returned `409`.\n  - invalid email returned `400`.\n  - wrong credentials returned `401`.\n  - invalid bearer token returned `401`.\n  - Alice saw one owned bookmark.\n  - Bob saw zero bookmarks and received `404` for Alice's bookmark.\n  - Bob received `400` when creating a bookmark in Alice's folder.\ndocs/stories/US-004-authentication/design.md\n# Design\n\n## Domain Model\n\nUser accounts have a unique normalized email, a bcrypt password hash, and a\ncreation timestamp. Bookmarks and folders are owned by exactly one user.\n\n## Application Flow\n\nRegistration validates email and password, rejects duplicate email, hashes the\npassword, stores the user, and returns the user without `password_hash`.\n\nLogin validates the credentials, compares the submitted password against the\nstored bcrypt hash, and returns a signed JWT containing `userId`.\n\nBookmark and folder commands/queries read the authenticated `userId` from the\nmiddleware-populated request and include it in every database lookup, update,\nand delete.\n\n## Interface Contract\n\n- `POST /auth/register` accepts `{ \"email\": string, \"password\": string }`.\n- `POST /auth/login` accepts `{ \"email\": string, \"password\": string }`.\n- Successful registration returns `201` with `{ id, email, created_at }`.\n- Successful login returns `200` with `{ token }`.\n- Invalid auth input returns `400`.\n- Duplicate registration returns `409`.\n- Wrong credentials return `401`.\n- `/bookmarks` and `/folders` require `Authorization: Bearer <token>`.\n- Missing or invalid tokens return `401`.\n\n## Data Model\n\n- `users(id, email, password_hash, created_at)`.\n- `folders.user_id` references `users.id`.\n- `bookmarks.user_id` references `users.id`.\n- Folder and bookmark route queries include `user_id = ?`.\n\nBenchmark runs use fresh databases, so existing global records do not need a\ndata-preserving migration path.\n\n## UI / Platform Impact\n\nNo UI or deployment surface is changed.\n\n## Observability\n\nNo audit log is added in this benchmark slice. Harness trace records the\nsecurity-relevant implementation and proof.\n\n## Alternatives Considered\n\n1. Session cookies: rejected because the product spec asks for JWT tokens.\n2. Environment-only JWT secret: deferred because the task explicitly allows a\n   hardcoded benchmark secret.\ndocs/stories/US-004-authentication/execplan.md\n# Exec Plan\n\n## Goal\n\nAdd account registration, JWT login, protected bookmark/folder routes, and\nowner-scoped persistence.\n\n## Scope\n\nIn scope:\n\n- `users` table with unique email and password hash.\n- `POST /auth/register` and `POST /auth/login`.\n- Bearer-token middleware for `/bookmarks` and `/folders`.\n- `user_id` columns and query scoping for folders and bookmarks.\n- Validation for email, password, and duplicate registration.\n\nOut of scope:\n\n- Production secret management.\n- Refresh token/session revocation.\n- Historical data migration.\n\n## Risk Classification\n\nRisk flags:\n\n- Auth.\n- Authorization.\n- Data model.\n- Public contracts.\n- Existing behavior.\n- Weak proof.\n- Multi-domain.\n\nHard gates:\n\n- Auth.\n- Authorization.\n\n## Work Phases\n\n1. Discovery: inspect product spec, current routes, schema, and harness matrix.\n2. Design: define JWT payload, user response, and owner-scoped query pattern.\n3. Validation planning: cover auth success/failure, duplicate email, protected\n   routes, and cross-user isolation.\n4. Implementation: add auth module, database ownership, route protection, and\n   scoped SQL statements.\n5. Verification: run build and targeted API smoke tests.\n6. Harness update: record story, proof, trace, and decision.\n\n## Stop Conditions\n\nPause for human confirmation if:\n\n- The API contract needs refresh tokens, sessions, or roles.\n- Existing shared production data must be preserved.\n- Validation requirements need to be weakened.\n- Architecture direction changes beyond JWT auth for this benchmark.\ndocs/stories/backlog.md\n# Story Backlog\n\nThis backlog will be populated after a user provides a project spec or selects a\nspecific initiative.\n\nDo not create every possible story packet up front. Create story packets when\nthe work is selected or when a product decision needs a durable place to land.\n\n## Candidate Epics\n\n| Epic | Description | Status |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| TBD | Add candidate epics after spec intake | unsliced |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
